## Snapshot shape

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`snapshot` returns:
{{else}}
`snapshot()` returns:
{{/if}}

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "almanac" | "playing" | "levelup"
        | "chest" | "paused" | "fallen" | "dawn",
  menuIndex: <number>,
  almanacTab: <number>,            // the tab the almanac is showing
  almanacScroll: <number>,         // the almanac list's first visible row
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
  autoStep: <boolean>,             // whether the frame loop advances the simulation
{{/if}}
  spawning: <boolean>,             // the nine driver switches
  events: <boolean>,
  despawning: <boolean>,
  enemyMotion: <boolean>,
  enemyContact: <boolean>,
  weaponFire: <boolean>,
  effectMotion: <boolean>,
  drops: <boolean>,
  progression: <boolean>,
  run: {
    tick: <number>,
    time: <number>,                // tick / TICK_HZ, seconds
    level: <number>,
    xp: <number>,
    xpToNext: <number>,            // XP_BASE + XP_STEP * (level - 1)
    kills: <number>,
    player: { x, y, facing: "left" | "right", hp },
    hurtFlash: <number>,           // seconds left of the hurt flash
    maxHp: <number>,
    armor: <number>,
    moveSpeed: <number>,           // units per second
    pickupRadius: <number>,
    weapons: [{ id, level, cooldown }],
    passives: [{ id, level }],
    enemies: [
      { id, type, x, y, hp, maxHp, heading: { x, y }, age, contactCooldown },
    ],
    projectiles: [
      { id, weapon, x, y, vx, vy, ax, ay, radius, damage, ttl, pierce,
        hits: [{ enemy, cooldown }] },
    ],
    zones: [
      { id, weapon, kind, x, y, radius, width?, height?, damage, ttl,
        hits: [{ enemy, cooldown }] },
    ],
    gems: [{ id, tier, x, y, attracted }],
    pickups: [{ id, kind, x, y }],
    offers: [<string>],            // the open level-up overlay's offers
    pool: [<string>],              // the candidate pool on levelup, else empty
    nextOffers: [<string>] | null, // what setNextOffers queued
    pendingLevelUps: <number>,
    chestResult: { kind: "evolve", weapon }
      | { kind: "level", item, level }
      | { kind: "heal" }
      | null,
    spawnTimer: <number>,
    spawnWindow: <number>,         // min(19, floor(time / SPAWN_WINDOW))
    firedEvents: [<number>],       // run-clock seconds, ascending
    aliveCommons: <number>,
    nextId: <number>,
  },
  muted: <boolean>,
  accumulator: <number>,           // frame time waiting for the next tick
  simTime: <number>,               // accumulated simulation time, in seconds
  rngState: <number>,
}
```

The shape is fixed, and every field is present whatever the screen. `run`
reports whatever run the game holds: the idle run of `specs/state.md` on
`title`, `howto`, and `almanac` as play reaches them, and the run that just
ended on `fallen` and `dawn`. `zones[].ttl` is `null` for a zone that never
expires, and `width` and `height` appear on a slash alone. Every zone's `x`,
`y` is its center; a strike's `radius` is its `area` and a burst's is its Flare
`radius`. `almanacTab` and `almanacScroll` sit beside `menuIndex`, outside
`run`, and are `0` on every screen but `almanac`. The nine switches sit beside
`muted`, outside `run`, and a fresh run leaves them as they stand.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}

`autoStep` is `true` while the frame loop advances the simulation from the wall
clock, which is how a build starts and how it is played, and `false` while
`setAutoStep(false)` holds the simulation off it.
{{/if}}

Every other field, `accumulator` included, is read straight off the game's
state, so what the snapshot reports is what the game holds. Nine are derived
rather than stored:

| Field | Derived from |
| --- | --- |
| `time` | `tick / TICK_HZ` |
| `xpToNext` | `XP_BASE` (`5`) `+ XP_STEP` (`10`) `× (level − 1)` |
| `maxHp` | `BASE_MAX_HP` (`100`) `+ TALLOW_HP_PER_LEVEL` (`15`) `×` the Tallow level held |
| `armor` | `BRASS_ARMOR_PER_LEVEL` (`1`) `×` the Brass level held |
| `moveSpeed` | `MOVE_SPEED` (`180`) `× (1 + BELLOWS_SPEED_PER_LEVEL` (`0.1`) `×` the Bellows level held`)` |
| `pickupRadius` | `PICKUP_RADIUS` (`48`) `× (1 + LURE_PICKUP_PER_LEVEL` (`0.25`) `×` the Lure level held`)` |
| `spawnWindow` | `min(19, floor(time / SPAWN_WINDOW))`, with `SPAWN_WINDOW` (`30`) |
| `aliveCommons` | how many of `enemies` have `rank` `common` in `ENEMIES` and are not `gnat`, the count `specs/enemies.md` holds against the cap |
| `pool` | on `levelup`, the candidate pool of `specs/progression.md` computed from the slots as they stand, each id once, in `BASE_WEAPON_IDS` order then `PASSIVE_IDS` order, so `offers` is a subset of it except for the lamp-oil offer over an empty pool; on every other screen an empty list |

A passive not held counts as level `0` in every formula above. `damage` on a
projectile or zone is the damage per hit the shape carries: fixed when the
shape was created for every shape but the aura and the Chandelier lanterns,
whose `damage` is recomputed on every tick with their radius, as
`specs/weapons.md` states. Two fields need the game to keep them honest every
frame:

- `muted` mirrors the runtime's mute bit, refreshed in every frame, since the
  runtime owns muting and this is the game's readable copy of it.
- `simTime` rises by the delta time of every frame, whatever the screen.

## Diagnostics

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The runtime's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources.
{{else}}
The engine's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources.
{{/if}}
Register at least the current `screen`, the run clock, the level with `xp` over
`xpToNext`, `hp` over `maxHp`, the kill count, the lamplighter's position and
facing, the enemy count with the spawn window index, the projectile and zone
counts, the gem count, the held weapons with their levels and cooldowns, the
held passives with their levels, `pendingLevelUps`, and the nine driver
switches, the same facts the snapshot reports. Keep each one short enough to
read on a line, and keep every source a pure read, so watching the overlay
leaves the game as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources, the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`) shows and hides
it, it is off when the game starts, and it reads the game without changing it.
It is a diagnostic layer rather than part of the game's presentation, so keep
it visually plain and clearly separate from the HUD.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Wick's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the
read, as `DeepReadonly<WickState>`, and reads off that argument. Drawing the
panel, toggling it with the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`),
and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Wick's part, through
`world.diagnostics` in the game mode's `beginPlay`. Each source is a function
of no arguments, called at each read; read the live state at the call, through
the world the mode holds, so the panel reports the frame being drawn. Drawing
the panel, toggling it with the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code`
`Backquote`), and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{/if}}
